%% The tangent space of a Riemannian manifold — a short visual lecture on a dome.
%% Build:
%%   python3 lmr2svg.py examples/tangent-space/deck.lmr -o tangent-space.html
!title The Tangent Space
!subtitle …or why a dome looks flat to an ant
!author lemur examples
!theme dark
!transition fade fade

!slide[.middle] An ant on a dome

An ant on a huge dome sees **flat ground** in every direction.

!pause

[Up close, curved looks flat.]{#ffd166} The **tangent space** makes that precise:
at every point $p$ of a curved surface $M$ sits a flat plane $T_pM$ — all the
directions in which you can set off from $p$.

!pause

And because it __is__ flat, we can do linear algebra there: add directions,
stretch them, measure angles.

!slide A curved world

!anim
	!src world.py

!slide What is a tangent vector?

Take any smooth curve on the surface that passes through $p$ and record its
velocity there:

:: math
	T_pM \;=\; \bigl\{\, \mk{vel}{\gamma'(0)} \;:\; \mk{curve}{\gamma\colon(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)\to M}, \ \mk{base}{\gamma(0)=p} \,\bigr\}

!annotate
	curve[#58c4dd]: any smooth curve on the surface …
	base[#ffd166]: … through the point $p$ …
	vel[#fc6255]: … and its velocity there

!pause

For a surface, $T_pM$ is a **plane**: a 2-dimensional vector space, glued to
$M$ at $p$.

!slide Every direction you can walk

!anim
	!src plane.py

!slide Riemannian: a ruler in every tangent plane

!definition Riemannian metric
	A smoothly varying inner product $g_p$ on every tangent space $T_pM$ — so
	we can measure lengths and angles of directions *at* $p$.

:: math
	g_p(u, v) = \langle u, v\rangle_{\mathbb{R}^3}, \qquad \|u\|_p = \sqrt{g_p(u,u)}

!pause

The dome simply inherits $g$ from the space around it. Lengths of curves follow,
$L(\gamma) = \int \|\gamma'(t)\|_{\gamma(t)}\,dt$, and the shortest paths are the
**geodesics** — here: arcs of great circles.

!slide Flattening the dome: $\log_p$

!anim
	!src logmap.py

!slide Two maps between the dome and the plane

:: math
	\mk{exp}{\exp_p(v)} = \mk{geo}{\gamma_v(1)} \qquad\qquad \mk{log}{\log_p(q)} = \exp_p^{-1}(q)

!annotate
	exp[#ffd166]: walk straight from $p$ in the direction $v$ …
	geo[#58c4dd]: … for exactly $\|v\|$ units of length
	log[#fc6255]: and back: which direction, how far, to reach $q$?

!pause

- $\exp_p$ turns straight lines through $p$ into **geodesics**
- $\log_p$ is the best flat map of the dome *as seen from $p$*: distances from $p$
  are exact — everything else is distorted, a little

!slide Shrink-wrapping the plane: $\exp_p$

!anim
	!src wrap.py

!slide Curvature is a circle that falls short

Walk a distance $r$ from $p$ in every direction. On a flat plane you trace a
circle of circumference $2\pi r$ — on the dome it comes out **shorter**:

:: math
	C(r) = 2\pi R \sin\frac{r}{R} = 2\pi r - \frac{\pi}{3}\,\frac{r^3}{R^2} + \dots

!pause

!theorem Bertrand–Diguet–Puiseux
	The shortfall of small geodesic circles measures the Gaussian curvature:
	:: math
		K = \lim_{r\to 0} \frac{3\,\bigl(2\pi r - C(r)\bigr)}{\pi\, r^3} = \frac{1}{R^2}

!slide A tangent space everywhere

!anim
	!src bundle.py

!slide[.middle] Take-aways

+ $T_pM$: the velocities of curves through $p$ — a flat vector space at every point
+ $g_p$: an inner product on each $T_pM$ — lengths, angles, geodesics
+ $\exp_p$ / $\log_p$: translate between the surface near $p$ and its tangent plane
+ curvature: how much the dome refuses to be flat — circles fall short

!pause

!style[.center]
	__Up close, every smooth world is flat. The interesting part is how the
	flat pieces fit together.__
